choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-King3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-King3)


ID: B3-Ch08b-King3
TEXT:

 The king of the underground and Albert trade another glance, and this time Albert takes the lead.

"We will see to prisoners with family living in the underground first," Albert says. "Once their safety is secured, we will evaluate how many more can be extracted without crippling the city's power."

"You plan to abandon anyone you do not personally care about," Kate says.

"No one is being abandoned," the king of the underground says. "They will be released in due course, but it demands patience. Outsiders struggle to grasp how thoroughly Ollendor relies on enchanted machinery. Many laborers commute hours on foot unless they take the aerial skiffs you saw before the lockdown, which cut the journey to minutes. If those skiffs drop from the sky, jobs vanish overnight and families starve. Would you take responsibility for shattering thousands of livelihoods simply to indulge your conscience with a handful of extra rescues?"

"Those 'extra rescues' have endured torture in those towers for months," Kate snaps, her fists tightening. "Do not dare equate their suffering to people complaining about walking an extra hour to work!"

"I recognize this strikes a nerve," the king of the underground says, meeting her glare without flinching. "I will grant that my illustration was simplistic. Yet your indignation blinds you to the broader picture. Transportation is merely the visible layer; commerce itself moves across Ollendor on those platforms. What happens when every contraption in the city grinds to a halt the day a new ruler ascends the throne? Mass unemployment leads directly to bread riots, and bread riots ignite civil war."

"So that is your final word," Kate says with open disgust. "You will leave them in cages to protect your grip on the throne."

The king of the underground closes his eyes and exhales slowly through his nose.

"This discussion runs in circles," the king of the underground says. "Think what you will. You have your answer. Shall we finally address the Overseer and his stranglehold on Ollendor?"

"We should," Daren says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Resistance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Resistance)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Kelrim2
TEXT:

 "Yes, yes, I am back," Horace says, waving off the crowd of rebels and refugees pressing forward to clasp his hand. "Drop the 'Sir,' I told you. My time in the royal guard ended years ago. Down here I am a rebel and a tunnel-dweller like the rest of you. We stand on equal ground. Though some, perhaps, stand a little higher than others."

Horace fixes his gaze directly on the king of the underground, challenging him to take the bait.

"Your popularity endures, Horace," the king of the underground says. "I confess I did not expect you beyond that threshold so soon. Your resilience never ceases to astonish me."

"Save it, 'Your Majesty,'" Horace says with a lopsided grin. "We both know my health is not what is eating at you. You want to know why I stepped past that door right now—especially after you dropped by my sickbed to politely request I keep out of sight until your little election wrapped up."

"The thought crossed my mind," the king of the underground admits.

 "I will be plain with you," Horace says. "I had no intention of interfering. I have stayed out of your way for years, knowing full well you dabble in murky waters, so long as the people down here saw the benefit. I might have let you stage your tidy little vote and crown yourself ruler of Ollendor. But then I heard Kelrim lamenting that no one in this city would ever back a goblin for the throne. Something in me snapped, and here I stand."

"Sentiment overrules reason once more," the king of the underground says. "Consider the reality. Presenting a goblin monarch to a populace reeling from the murder of their king will look like surrender to beasts. Good intentions count for nothing. They will demand his head on a pike before the ink on the proclamation dries."

"Horace, I am grateful for your support, but I cannot do this," Kelrim says, wringing his small green hands. "I am a healer, not a statesman or a battle commander. I possess no gift for leadership. I would have no idea where to start."

 "Then I will stand at your shoulder," Horace says. "Call me your right hand. It is the least I can offer after everything you did to keep me breathing these past weeks. Put competence out of your head for now. The only question that matters is this: do you want the crown?"

"Do I want... the crown?" Kelrim repeats, his eyes widening as the weight of the idea settles in.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Kelrim3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Kelrim3)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Kelrim3
TEXT:

 "His desire is irrelevant," the king of the underground says, his calm veneer cracking for the first time. "Even with your backing, no one will cast a ballot for him. At best, they see him as a figurehead with you pulling the strings. If that is your play, drop the pretense. Challenge me openly. I would rather face you on the ballot than watch you hide behind a goblin healer. No sensible citizen votes for Kelrim unless--"

"Did I hear that right?" a rebel calls out, jogging over. "Kelrim is standing for king?"

"No," the king of the underground snaps, his voice sharper than usual. "We are discussing hypothetical candidates. No one declared--"

"Kelrim on the ballot?" another man shouts, pushing through the crowd. "He has my vote! My wife would be in a grave without his draughts. He saved this entire cavern!"

"Did you hear?" a murmur ripples through a cluster of fighters nearby. "Kelrim is entering the race. Sir Horace pledged to back him."

 "A goblin on the throne?" a refugee replies. "Best notion I have heard all week."

The spark catches and runs through the chamber. Within moments, rebels and refugees swarm around our circle, clamoring to grasp Kelrim's sleeve. Plague survivors, kin of the recovered, and laborers he patched up after cave-ins press in from every corridor. The hall buzzes with shouting voices and shuffling boots as curiosity draws the rest of the encampment into the crush.

"Kelrim?" a refugee asks near the outer ring. "The small fellow who worked until he dropped when the guards brought the ceiling down? He is running? That settles my vote."

"What of the fellow calling himself king of the underground?" a woman asks. "Is he not on the slate?"

 "That schemer?" the first man scoffs. "Who votes for a shadow boss when the healer is on the table?"

I glance toward the king of the underground to see how the blow lands. The man remains stone. A tight set to his jaw is the only giveaway; to anyone unfamiliar with his tells, he looks entirely detached from the noise.

Credit where it is due: the man possesses formidable discipline.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Kelrim4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Kelrim4)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Kelrim4
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch8_unusual,1)
achievement("An unusual candidate",v_ac_b3_ch8_unusual)
 "So, what do you say, Kelrim?" Horace asks, a grin spreading across his battered face. "Will you do us the honor of putting your name forward?"

"I..." Kelrim looks around the sea of cheering faces, his voice catching in his throat. "I would not wish to overstep... only if the king of the underground consents..."

"Hear that, 'Your Majesty'?" Horace calls over the clamor. "Kelrim asks your blessing. Do you grant it?"

"Of course," the king of the underground says with a measured smile. "The ballot is open to any citizen. Fairness demands nothing less."

 "Settled, then," Horace announces. "Come along, Kelrim. Let these gentlemen conclude their business in peace. We have details to hammer out, and my legs are about to buckle. Into the ward. Clear a path, everyone! You will have ample opportunity to cheer for him before the vote. Make way!"

Horace steers Kelrim back through the quarantine doors. The crowd slowly drifts apart, leaving our group standing with the king of the underground, Albert, and Peter.

"Now then," the king of the underground says, turning back to us with practiced composure. "You have your answer regarding candidates for the throne. Shall we proceed to the pressing matter of the Overseer and our plan to neutralize him?"

#if(v_b3_ch8_next == 2) {
 As the question hangs in the air, it strikes me that I never pressed him on what happens to the seredium tower prisoners. Then again, with Kelrim and Horace spearheading the government, the decision will likely rest in their hands anyway.

}
choice(""Actually, I was hoping we could talk some more about your humiliating defeat."", B3-Ch08b-Developments, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Developments, v_b3_ch8_defeat = 0)
choice(""Just one quick question. Did you and Horace serve as royal guards at the same time?"", B3-Ch08b-Developments, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Developments, v_b3_ch8_defeat = 1)
choice(""Yeah, we should probably talk about that."", B3-Ch08b-Developments, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Developments, v_b3_ch8_defeat = 2)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Developments
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_defeat == 0) {
 "Actually, I was hoping we could talk some more about your humiliating defeat," I say. "Perhaps rub a little salt in the cut?"

"Yes, tell us more about how 'no sensible citizen' votes for a goblin," Arraka's voice screeches from the amulet, multiple laughter trails overlapping in mock glee. "Next to you, a literal cave monster sounds like paradise to these primitives! Ahahahahaha!"

Hadrik barks a laugh that rumbles off the cavern ceiling, joining Arraka's cackling until Daren steps between them and the king of the underground.

"Pardon my companions," Daren says, holding up a hand. "They have a habit of--"

"No offense taken," the king of the underground says, his smile tight and brittle. "I have grown familiar with your companions' temperaments. Their antics do not surprise me. Now that they have had their amusement, however, we must address the Overseer and his grip on Ollendor."

"We should," Daren says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_defeat == 1) {
 "Just one quick question. Did you and Horace serve as royal guards at the same time?"

"We did," the king of the underground says. "And we defected in tandem. We attempted to bring others with us, including the captain of the guard, but our perspectives diverged. We retreated into these caverns and built this society stone by stone. Years have passed since those days, and I have no desire to dwell on old history. If your curiosity is satisfied, can we turn to the Overseer?"

"We should," Daren says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_defeat == 2) {
 "Yeah, we should probably talk about that."

}
 "Let us proceed," the king of the underground says. "Allow me to brief you on recent developments aboveground since your return from the seredium tower. Our scouts report relentless activity from the Overseer. Following the monarch's death, his first move was the targeted assassination of every high-ranking official aware of his central core. Daren, Peter briefed you on the core's existence. Have you shared that with your allies?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Resistance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Resistance)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Resistance
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_watching == 0 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
 "Let us proceed," the king of the underground says. "Allow me to brief you on recent developments aboveground since your return from the seredium tower. Our scouts report relentless activity from the Overseer. Following the monarch's death, his first move was the targeted assassination of every high-ranking official aware of his central core. Daren, Peter briefed you on the core's existence. Have you shared that with your allies?"

"Yes," Daren says. "They know."

"Good," the king of the underground says. "We launch an assault against that core tomorrow, once our forces are marshaled. May I assume you will fight alongside us?"

"We already agreed to march with you tomorrow," Daren says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_watching == 1 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
 "Let us proceed," the king of the underground says. "Allow me to brief you on recent developments aboveground since your return from the seredium tower. Our scouts report relentless activity from the Overseer. Following the monarch's death, his first move was the targeted assassination of every high-ranking official aware of his central core. Daren, Peter briefed you on the core's existence. Have you shared that with your allies?"

"They know we plan to strike the Overseer, but not the specifics," Daren says.

"Very well," the king of the underground says. "In short, a single central apparatus directs every floating drone across the city, and I know its coordinates. We are gathering every capable fighter for a dawn strike before he fortifies his position further. Since you already discussed our intent, can we count on your swords tomorrow?"

"We already agreed to march with you tomorrow," Daren says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_watching == 0) {
 "Yes," Daren says. "They know."

"Good," the king of the underground says. "We launch an assault against that core tomorrow, once our forces are marshaled. May I assume you will fight alongside us?"

"We already agreed to march with you tomorrow," Daren says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_watching == 1) {
 "They know we plan to strike the Overseer, but not the specifics," Daren says.

"Very well," the king of the underground says. "In short, a single central apparatus directs every floating drone across the city, and I know its coordinates. We are gathering every capable fighter for a dawn strike before he fortifies his position further. Since you already discussed our intent, can we count on your swords tomorrow?"

"We already agreed to march with you tomorrow," Daren says.

}
 "Excellent," the king of the underground says. "Detailed assignments will follow once our roster is finalized. My hope is that the Overseer remains ignorant of our knowledge regarding his core. If he anticipates our strike, we will face heavy resistance en route. Should that prove true, our only recourse is to cut straight through his perimeter. We are committing our finest warriors; there is no holding back. Note also that the citywide lockdown remains enforced. His metal spheres will engage on sight the moment we breach the surface, regardless of provocation."

"Understood," Daren says. 

 "One further caution," the king of the underground adds, lowering his voice. "Refrain from discussing our assault near any blue crystals. Albert reported an impression in the throne room that we were being observed through the mineral veins. When pressed, he described it as a sensory instinct rather than a foreign aura detected by magical sense. I trust his instincts implicitly. The Overseer is the logical culprit—his floating spheres house blue crystals within their chassis, suggesting a resonance network through the cavern walls. That resonance may also account for the crystals' recent instability, though the shockwaves from the rebel explosives could share the blame."

choice(""You're right about the Overseer. We detected him through one of the crystals, earlier."", B3-Ch08b-Populated, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Populated, v_b3_ch8_detected = 1)
choice(""That's good to know. We'll try to be more careful around these crystals from now on."", B3-Ch08b-Populated, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Populated, v_b3_ch8_detected = 0)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Populated
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_detected == 1) {
 "You are right about the Overseer," I say. "We caught him listening through a crystal node not an hour ago."

"You detected him?..." The king's brow arches, his gaze sharpening. "I suppose I should not find that entirely unexpected, given your banshee claimed she could pierce Albert's cloaking. You are certain the transmission originated from the machine?"

"No question," I say. "He spoke to us directly."

"Then I will withhold operational specifics until the dawn muster," the king says, his jaw setting. "I cannot have an operative compromising the assault because they failed to notice a cerulean vein above a doorframe. My thanks for the warning. As promised, detailed orders will follow when the roster forms at daybreak. Until then, the caverns and their stores remain open to you."

"We will keep that in mind," Daren says, offering a formal nod. "Until tomorrow, then."

The king turns back toward his officers, and we follow Daren out of the waiting hall into a narrow side passage where blast concussions sheared the blue crystal veins down to dead rock. Once the torchlit commons are far behind us and our footsteps ring only against cold granite, Hadrik breaks the silence.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_detected == 0) {
 "Good to know," I say. "We will watch what we say around the stone."

"Prudent," the king says. "That concludes our preliminary business. As promised, detailed orders will follow when the roster forms at daybreak. Until then, the caverns and their stores remain open to you, just as before."

"We will keep that in mind," Daren says, offering a formal nod. "Until tomorrow, then."

The king turns back toward his officers, and we follow Daren out of the waiting hall into a narrow side passage where blast concussions sheared the blue crystal veins down to dead rock. Once the torchlit commons are far behind us and our footsteps ring only against cold granite, Hadrik breaks the silence.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0) {
 "Did anyone else notice how easily he swallowed Barry putting Albert on the ballot?" Hadrik asks, scratching through his dark beard. "I expected him to bare his teeth the second we floated another name."

"Why would he?" Kate asks, stepping alongside him, her green cloak pulled close against the cavern draft. "Albert is his creature. A crown on Albert's head changes nothing—the orders will still come from the shadows, and Albert will still carry them out. In practical terms, the underground king rules whether his own brow wears the gold or his executioner's does."

"Even so," Hadrik mutters, "that recording made it sound like he would slit our throats the instant we contested his claim. You would think he would have offered some bluster about being the rightful ruler before rolling over. It smells crooked. Could that crystal recording have been fabricated? We could confront him directly and let Melindra or Leila read his aura while he answers."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 1) {
 "Did anyone else notice how quickly he agreed to put Peter on the slate?" Hadrik asks, scratching through his dark beard. "I expected him to bare his teeth the second we floated another name."

"He believes Peter is easily steered," Kate says, her dark eyes cold in the dim torchlight. "We have all watched Peter shrink the moment the king raises his voice. If Peter takes the throne on popular acclaim, he might find the spine to govern, but the king is banking on keeping his leash firmly in hand."

"Even so," Hadrik mutters, "that recording made it sound like he would slit our throats the instant we contested his claim. You would think he would have offered some bluster about being the rightful ruler before rolling over. It smells crooked. Could that crystal recording have been fabricated? We could confront him directly and let Melindra or Leila read his aura while he answers."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 2) {
 "Did anyone else notice how little resistance he put up against Breynan?" Hadrik asks, scratching through his dark beard. "I expected him to bare his teeth the second we floated another name."

"He knows Breynan commands no broad following," Kate says, her tone crisp and dry. "Breynan has the stomach for logistics, but none of the warmth that wins a crowd. The king will not even need to manipulate the ballots; running against an austere rebel commander simply legitimizes his own landslide."

"Even so," Hadrik mutters, "that recording made it sound like he would slit our throats the instant we contested his claim. You would think he would have offered some bluster about being the rightful ruler before rolling over. It smells crooked. Could that crystal recording have been fabricated? We could confront him directly and let Melindra or Leila read his aura while he answers."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 3 || v_b3_ch8_king == 4) {
 "Did anyone else find it strange how readily he let Daren propose Leila's father?" Hadrik asks, scratching through his dark beard. "I expected him to bare his teeth the second we floated another name."

"He knew doctor Reidzek would decline before Daren finished the sentence," Kate says, her boots scuffing the rough floor. "He had nothing to lose by playing the gracious statesman."

"Even so," Hadrik mutters, "that recording made it sound like he would slit our throats the instant we contested his claim. You would think he would have offered some bluster about being the rightful ruler before letting Daren speak. It smells crooked. Could that crystal recording have been fabricated? We could confront him directly and let Melindra or Leila read his aura while he answers."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5) {
 "Did anyone else catch how quietly he took that defeat?" Hadrik asks, rubbing his knuckles. "I expected him to fight harder to keep Kelrim off the slate."

"He could not object openly without exposing his fear of a fair vote," Kate says, her voice low and sharp. "Do not mistake composure for surrender. He will move against Kelrim the moment the crowds disperse—and against us, if we stand in the way. We heard his orders to Albert. The man will not permit a goblin healer to take the prize he spent years scheming for."

"He was certainly displeased by the notion of a non-human on the throne," doctor Reidzek says, adjusting his spectacles with a frown. "Yet would he truly slaughter his own allies over an election?"

 "Kate is right, Doctor," Daren says, his heavy hand resting on his pommel. "The roar in that hall made it clear: he cannot beat Kelrim in an honest vote. He knows it, and Albert knows it. Our alliance with the underground broke the moment Horace walked through that door. We either accept the Overseer's terms to strike him down, or we face him ourselves when the dust settles above. Wait—did anyone confirm whether Albert followed us cloaked?"

"Relax," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet. "If that shadow were tailing you, I would have made a game of his panic."

"Right," Daren says, scanning the empty shadows ahead with lingering doubt. "In any case, we need clear heads before we choose our path. We split up, continue our preparations, and reconvene at our quarters before dusk. I will call through the transceivers when the time comes."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 0 || v_b3_ch8_king == 1 || v_b3_ch8_king == 2 || v_b3_ch8_king == 3 || v_b3_ch8_king == 4) {
 "Leila explained this to me," Kate says, shaking her head. "An aura read catches direct falsehoods, but the king trades entirely in omissions and technical truths. He shapes every sentence so the words are factual while the implication is rotten. Pressing him for an aura check would only hand him another stage to deceive us."

"His aura discipline is thoroughly irritating," Arraka sneers from the gold casing. "He has trained a deliberate tremor into his resonance whenever he speaks an undisputed truth, muddying the baseline for any observer. Differentiating his genuine fabrications from his manufactured twitches takes more focus than the creature is worth. I have no intention of wasting my talents dissecting a petty mobster's pulse."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Workshop, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Workshop)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Workshop
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5) {
 "Hold on," Hadrik says, stopping in his tracks. "When we forged our compact with the Overseer, he stipulated Breynan as the only acceptable sovereign. What happens when he discovers we backed a goblin he never budgeted for?"

"The agreement will require renegotiation," Daren says firmly. "We accepted his governance behind the scenes when no alternative existed. With Kelrim carrying the people's trust, I will not reduce him to a mechanical puppet. If the Overseer refuses to share authority with Kelrim, the pact is void."

"Agreed," I say. "We consult Breynan first, gauge his stance on Kelrim's surge, and then present our revised terms to the machine. But Daren is right—we need to gather our strength before we open that door."

"Suits me," Hadrik says, rolling his broad shoulders. "I have a new shifting sequence to drill into muscle memory before morning. See you at dusk."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
 "Suits me," Hadrik says, rolling his broad shoulders. "I have a new shifting sequence to drill into muscle memory before morning. See you at dusk."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
 "Even if we could pierce his defenses," Daren says, "revealing our contact with the Overseer would instantly sever whatever trust remains. The underground king suspects treason behind every shadow; learning we parleyed with his mortal enemy inside his own fortress would turn him hostile on the spot. We must weigh our allegiance carefully and choose our banner before nightfall. Wait—did anyone verify whether Albert shadowed us cloaked?"

"Relax," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet. "If that shadow were tailing you, I would have made a game of his panic."

"Right," Daren says, scanning the passage with lingering doubt. "In any case, we need clear heads before we choose our path. We split up, continue our preparations, and reconvene at our quarters before dusk. I will inform Breynan privately so he can weigh the Overseer's offer with us."

"Suits me," Hadrik says, rolling his broad shoulders. "I have a new shifting sequence to drill into muscle memory before morning. See you at dusk."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
 "Even if we could pierce his defenses," Daren says, "revealing our contact with the Overseer would instantly sever whatever trust remains. The underground king suspects treason behind every shadow; learning we parleyed with his mortal enemy inside his own fortress would turn him hostile on the spot. We must weigh our allegiance carefully and choose our banner before nightfall. Wait—did anyone verify whether Albert shadowed us cloaked?"

"Relax," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet. "If that shadow were tailing you, I would have made a game of his panic."

"Right," Daren says, scanning the passage with lingering doubt. "In any case, we need clear heads before we choose our path. We split up, continue our preparations, and reconvene at our quarters before dusk. I will call through the transceivers when the hour arrives."

"Suits me," Hadrik says, rolling his broad shoulders. "I have a new shifting sequence to drill into muscle memory before morning. See you at dusk."

}
 "In that case, I will resume my lessons with Kate," Melindra says, resting a hand on her hip. "Flower, come along. Stillwater channeling operates on principles that defy conventional elemental frameworks. I need your intuition to bridge the theory into terms she can use."

"Right behind you!" Flower says, her eyes brightening. "Which corridor?"

"The abandoned gallery we reinforced yesterday," Melindra says. "Assuming the earth mages finished the shoring."

"Understood," Flower says with a nod. "Lead the way."

"I am going to walk the perimeter," I say as Kate, Flower, and Melindra head down the western branch. "If the base is leaning toward revolt or coronation, I want to hear it before we cast our vote."

 "Keep your hood up and your distance clear," Daren warns quietly. "Do not draw their enforcers."

"Understood," I say.

"I must return to the workbench," doctor Reidzek says, dusting stone powder from his leather apron. "Several enchantments require stabilization before tomorrow's operation. Leila, will you assist me with the alignment arrays?"

"Of course," Leila writes, bright blue characters flaring briefly beside her hand before fading.

"I will accompany you toward the waiting cavern," Daren tells them, adjusting his sword belt. "A few refugees in the clinic corridor had bandaging that needed fresh binding. Lead on, Doctor."

I watch doctor Reidzek, Leila, and Daren retrace their steps toward the main hall, then turn south toward the common galleries.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Workshop2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Workshop2)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Workshop2
TEXT:

 I spend the next few hours moving through the cavern network, reading the pulse of the encampment. I loop through the mess hall where thin broth and hard ration loaves are served over scarred wooden trestles, through the bunk galleries where fighters oil their chainmail, and into the commons where surface refugees huddle near charcoal braziers.

In almost every corner, Albert's operatives circulate among the fires, recounting the underground king's victories and pressing the necessity of a strong hand on the throne. Yet skepticism runs deep. A substantial faction of seasoned rebels bristles under his autocratic grip and would rally behind any credible alternative. Among the refugees, his reputation is darker still.

To the families who fled the surface, he is less a liberator than an extortionist and guildmaster of thieves—a perception reinforced by his self-appointed title and history of quiet disappearances. In the darker alcoves, they speak of him as king of the 'underworld,' trading anxious whispers about what his reign will bring once the old monarch's seat is his.

Between circuits, I check in on our companions. In a side gallery, I find Kate and Arraka locked in a sharp cosmological debate. Kate argues that because the same mortal races walk both the earthen and magical planes, both realms must have diverged from a shared primordial ancestry.

Arraka scoffs from the gold casing, dismissing the deduction as primate logic. She counters that common racial forms offer zero proof of shared origin, especially when the Magium actively generates new magical spirits conforming to those identical racial templates.

In Arraka's framework, the division of the primordial plane was an uncalculated disaster, and the Magium functions like a desperate crew shoring up a foundering vessel—rationing spirit creation on the magical plane while dispatching time weavers across the earthen plane to forestall mass deaths that could tear reality apart.

 She adds that modern mortal and spirit races bear no resemblance to the entities that walked the primordial plane, having emerged as anomalous byproducts in the cataclysm's wake.

I leave them to their argument, with Kate looking thoroughly unconvinced by the banshee's cosmology.

After brief checks on Hadrik drilling stance transitions, Daren wrapping fresh dressings in the clinic corridor, and Leila calibrating alignment pins with her father, dusk approaches. Daren's voice crackles over the transceivers, calling us back to an abandoned, crystal-free gallery to settle our final stance.

The debate in the narrow tunnel is fierce, with every perspective clashing against the stone, but after weighing the survival of Ollendor against the ruthlessness of both factions, we forge our consensus...

choice("Agree with the Overseer's plan of setting an ambush for the king of the underground.", B3-Ch08b-Answer, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Answer, v_b3_ch8_overseer2 = 1)
choice("Tell the Overseer that we won't help him set an ambush for the king of the underground.", B3-Ch08b-Answer, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Answer, v_b3_ch8_overseer2 = 0)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Answer
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
 ...tell the Overseer that we won't help him set an ambush for the king of the underground.

With our stance determined, we return to our quarters and speak his name. A cerulean node in the granite flares to life almost instantly, as though his transmission had remained queued throughout our deliberation.

When Daren delivers our refusal, the Overseer's flat tone carries a sharp, metallic edge. Even so, his logic subroutines calculate a margin for error, promising his aerial drones will hold fire during our initial breach of the surface to grant us a final window to eliminate the underground king ourselves.

With the link severed, we turn to our bedrolls as night settles over the base.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
 ...tell the Overseer that we won't help him set an ambush for the king of the underground.

With our stance determined, we return to our quarters and speak his name. A cerulean node in the granite flares to life almost instantly, as though his transmission had remained queued throughout our deliberation.

When Daren delivers our refusal, the Overseer's flat tone carries a sharp, metallic edge. Even so, his logic subroutines calculate a margin for error, promising his aerial drones will hold fire during our initial breach of the surface to grant us a final window to eliminate the underground king ourselves.

With the battle lines drawn for dawn, Breynan returns to his post, and we request that the Overseer terminate surveillance through the rock face so we can sleep.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 9 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 || v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_b3_ch4_billy <= 6 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
 ...agree with the Overseer's plan of setting an ambush for the king of the underground.

With our course settled, we return to our quarters and speak his name. A cerulean node in the granite pulses to life instantly, the transmission ready and waiting.

We formulate our terms with surgical care, exploiting the rigid prohibition against direct falsehoods embedded in his architecture.

Daren binds him to an absolute pledge: the underground king and his officers will be secured alive inside paralysis containment, unharmed throughout their detention. We further stipulate that once the rebel leadership is in custody, the Overseer will terminate the municipal lockdown and guarantee our party unmolested departure beyond the perimeter walls, provided we initiate no hostile actions against his units.

On the seredium tower mages, his logic remains unyielding. The captive spellcasters supply the power grid driving the city's transport platforms and municipal machinery; he refuses their release until alternate energy sources of equal output are integrated.

 Recognizing the technical barrier, we table the demand for the moment, resolving to force the issue once the king is secured.

With the verbal compact ratified, we demand that the Overseer withdraw his monitoring from the cavern walls so we can rest.

"Does anyone want to volunteer for a night shift?" Daren asks as we lay out our bedrolls.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
 ...agree with the Overseer's plan of setting an ambush for the king of the underground.

With our course settled, we return to our quarters and speak his name. A cerulean node in the granite pulses to life instantly, the transmission ready and waiting.

Before confirming our alliance, we have the Overseer replay the intercepted recording of the underground king ordering Albert to eliminate us should we challenge his claim. Breynan reels as the words echo through the stone, forced to accept that peaceful reform under the king was an illusion.

With consensus established, we bind the Overseer to a formal arrangement: he will share civic administration with Breynan, with the immediate objective of overhauling the seredium towers so the captive mages can be freed without collapsing the city's power grid.

 Breynan departs for his quarters to prepare his loyalists, and we instruct the Overseer to halt his surveillance so we can sleep.

"Does anyone want to volunteer for a night shift?" Daren asks as we lay out our bedrolls.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
 ...agree with the Overseer's plan of setting an ambush for the king of the underground.

With our course settled, we return to our quarters and speak his name. A cerulean node in the granite pulses to life instantly, the transmission ready and waiting.

When we inform him that Kelrim will take the throne rather than Breynan, the Overseer's monotone sharpens with open resistance, stating that municipal governance must be renegotiated once the underground king is neutralized.

We also have the Overseer replay the recording of the king instructing Albert to assassinate us, dispelling whatever lingering hesitation Breynan harbored regarding an alliance against his sovereign.

Once the tactical parameters for the surface breach are aligned, Breynan leaves to brief his inner circle, and we instruct the Overseer to halt his surveillance so we can rest.

}
