ID: B3-Ch08b-Hadrik
TEXT:

 "Actually, yes," Leila writes, blue characters shimmering before her fingers. "Barry mentioned earlier that he wanted to take a watch with Hadrik to keep him company. Isn't that right, Barry?"

"Right," I say, catching her eye. "Good thing you remembered. Would you be open to taking the first watch together, Hadrik?"

Hadrik blinks, slowly lowering the strap of his backpack. The room falls quiet, every face turning toward me.

"Did I say something out of line?" I ask.

"No," Kate says dryly from the corner where she is rolling her cloak into a pillow. "Daren offered nearly the exact same proposal ten minutes before you walked in. He wanted to share a drink with Hadrik to lift his spirits, and the two of them had already claimed the opening shift."

choice("Take the first night shift together with both Daren and Hadrik.", B3-Ch08b-Hadrik2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Hadrik2, v_b3_ch8_three = 1)
choice("Let Daren and Hadrik take the first night shift, and do the second night shift with Leila.", B3-Ch08b-Hadrik-Leila, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Hadrik-Leila, v_b3_ch8_three = 0)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Hadrik-Leila
TEXT:

 "In that case," I say, "three people on one watch is a waste of sleep. I can share a drink with Hadrik another time. Leila, do you still want to take a shift together? We could volunteer for the second watch tonight."

"Sounds like a plan," Leila writes with a nod.

"Settled, then," I say.

"Well now," Hadrik rumbles, a broad grin splitting his black beard. "Two grown men offering to cheer me over drink on the very same evening. I must have been a miserable sight today. That ends right now. Daren, prepare yourself—we are holding a proper northern drinking bout tonight. No retreats!"

"If your bout means measured sips spaced across several hours, I agree," Daren says calmly.

"Hahaha!" Hadrik's laugh bounces off the stone. "Always the legend! You will see sense once the first bottle breathes. Come along—my pack is heavy with vintage."

 As Daren and Hadrik step out into the corridor with their gear, the rest of us settle onto our bedrolls. I manage about five hours of deep, dreamless sleep before Daren's heavy gauntlet nudges my shoulder. Leila is already on her feet, adjusting her dagger sheaths. We make our way down the quiet gallery to the watch post Daren and Hadrik vacated, sitting with our backs braced against the cold rock wall.

Just like our previous night watches, conversation flows with surprising ease. Over the next few hours we move effortlessly through arcane theory, the habits of northern beasts, and the finer points of her father's enchantment alignment arrays. Despite the technical density of the topics, my focus never wavers for a second.

 "There is something I have wondered about for a while," I say quietly, looking over at her as she sits with her knees drawn to her chest. "Whenever you are alone with me or Kate, your writing is quick and fluent. But the moment the whole group sits together, you barely write a word. Are you uncomfortable around the others, or is it larger groups in general?"

"It is... groups in general," Leila writes after a brief hesitation, her fingers tracing the glowing blue letters in midair. "At the Beacon I had no device, so I grew used to sitting in corners in complete silence while others spoke. Since escaping, it has mostly been just my father and me. Stepping into a group conversation always feels like trying to jump into a running river."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Leila2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Leila2)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Leila
TEXT:

 "Barry and I will take the second watch, if that works for everyone," Leila writes.

"Works well," Daren says. "Hadrik, you are still comfortable opening the watch with me?"

"More than comfortable," Hadrik says, clapping him on the shoulder. "Dwarven ale burns clean, but an extra watch of sleep before dawn will clear whatever cobwebs remain."

"Just keep the bottles quiet while the rest of us rest," Daren says with a faint smile. "Get your gear. We will take the bend where the blast cleared the crystal veins."

As Daren and Hadrik step into the corridor, the rest of us settle onto our blankets. I manage roughly five hours of deep sleep before Daren's hand shakes my shoulder awake. Leila is already waiting, her daggers secured and her boots laced. We take over the watch post, sitting shoulder to shoulder with our backs braced against the solid granite wall.

 Just like our previous night watches, conversation flows with surprising ease. Over the next few hours we move effortlessly through arcane theory, the habits of northern beasts, and the finer points of her father's enchantment alignment arrays. Despite the technical density of the topics, my focus never wavers for a second.

"There is something I have wondered about for a while," I say quietly, looking over at her as she sits with her knees drawn to her chest. "Whenever you are alone with me or Kate, your writing is quick and fluent. But the moment the whole group sits together, you barely write a word. Are you uncomfortable around the others, or is it larger groups in general?"

 "It is... groups in general," Leila writes after a brief hesitation, her fingers tracing the glowing blue letters in midair. "At the Beacon I had no device, so I grew used to sitting in corners in complete silence while others spoke. Since escaping, it has mostly been just my father and me. Stepping into a group conversation always feels like trying to jump into a running river."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Leila2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Leila2)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Leila2
TEXT:

 "I understand," I say. "No one holds it against you. Still, it is a pity doctor Reidzek cannot configure your device to generate an audible voice alongside the script. It would let you join in naturally without feeling like an intruder."

"Yes!" Leila writes, her posture straightening with sudden energy. "That is exactly the feeling—like every sentence I put forward is an interruption."

"It makes sense mechanically," I say. "Even with a responsive device translating thought into glyphs, there is a delay between forming an idea and materializing the text. You also have to position the script so everyone can read it without blocking their line of sight. By the time you calibrate the size and angle, the conversation has already moved three topics ahead."

"Exactly," Leila writes, extending her legs and leaning her head back against the stone. "If I place the writing too low, someone misses it; if I place it in the center, it feels intrusive. Having a real voice would change everything..."

 "Well," I say, "I cannot build you an acoustic transmitter, but for what it is worth, there is at least one person who hears a voice when you write."

"...What do you mean?" Leila writes, tilting her head with a puzzled look.

"The voice from the ethereal plane," I say. "When the Magium fragment hurled us across dimensions and your thoughts reached me directly. Ever since that moment, whenever I read your glowing text, my mind reads it in that voice. Even knowing it was an ethereal projection, the impression stuck—hey, are you alright? Your face is completely red."

"I... almost forgot you could hear my inner voice during that storm..." Leila writes, the blue script trembling and blurring slightly as blood rushes to her cheeks.

 "Your inner voice?" I ask, studying her. "You mean that was the voice you hear in your own head when you think?"

Leila does not write. She gives a small, bashful nod, her gaze dropping to her boots.

"How?" I ask gently. "You have never heard your own voice spoken aloud, have you?"

"I have no physical voice," Leila writes, the flush slowly subsiding as she collects herself. "When I was young, watching other children speak made me want one of my own. So I pieced one together in my mind, borrowing tones from people around me and aging the timbre as the years passed. The voice you heard in the ether was the current version—the one I use to think right now. I never imagined anyone else in the world would ever hear it, so talking about it feels strange..."

choice(""Well, I think your voice sounds wonderful!"", B3-Ch08b-Leila3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Leila3, v_b3_ch8_voice = 1)
choice(""Ah, so that is the reason why your voice sounded so artificial and unnatural!"", B3-Ch08b-Leila3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Leila3, v_b3_ch8_voice = 0)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Leila3
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_voice == 1) {
 "Well, I think your voice sounds wonderful!"

"Thank you..." Leila writes, looking up with a soft, shy smile.

"Would you mind telling me how you built it?" I ask. "Or is that too private?"

"Not at all," Leila writes. "There was no grand formula. I listened to girls near my age in the towns we passed through, took the cadences I liked best, and shaped them into something that felt right. What about you? Does the voice in your head match the one you speak with? Did you shape it deliberately, or did it develop on its own?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_voice == 0) {
 "Ah, so that is the reason why your voice sounded so artificial and unnatural!"

"Nice try," Leila writes with a teasing glint in her eyes. "If it were truly so unnatural, why have you spent the last five hours reading all my words with it?"

"Well... that is because..." I trail off, caught out. "Fair point. You have me."

"Always," Leila writes with a quiet grin.

"In all seriousness," I say, "how did you assemble it? Or is that too personal?"

"Not at all," Leila writes. "There was no grand formula. I listened to girls near my age in the towns we passed through, took the cadences I liked best, and shaped them into something that felt right. What about you? Does the voice in your head match the one you speak with? Did you shape it deliberately, or did it develop on its own?"

}
 "Hmm," I say, rubbing my chin as I turn the question over. "Now that you bring it up... what voice do I use? As a child I thought in a higher pitch, but when my speaking voice deepened, did my internal cadence follow immediately or lag behind? Did I ever consciously adjust the resonance, or did it settle unnoticed? Why have I never analyzed this until tonight?"

As I frown at the ceiling trying to trace the mechanics, Leila's shoulders shake with silent laughter.

"Did I say something absurd?" I ask.

 "No," Leila writes, trying and failing to stifle her amusement. "It is just... watching you overthink this out loud is exactly how your thoughts sounded when we were tumbling through the planes. You were running theoretical deductions even while we were being hurled across the void!"

"In my defense," I say with a faint grin, "analytical theory is how I stay alive. Overthinking is practically a survival reflex."

"I suppose it is," Leila writes, her smile warm as her amusement settles.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Leila4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Leila4)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Leila4
TEXT:

 A quiet stillness settles over the corridor. Leila gazes at the rough granite opposite us, her expression softening into solemn contemplation before fresh script forms between us.

"We have survived a great deal together, haven't we?..." the blue writing says. "Fighting side by side in the arena, hearing each other's thoughts, holding onto each other across the planes so the void wouldn't take us..."

The text dissolves, leaving a heavy silence between us. We skirted the memory all night, and feeling the gravity of it now, the hesitation makes complete sense.

 "I was terrified when it happened," Leila writes, breaking the quiet. "I kept my hands steady, but inside... I have never felt a terror like that in my life."

"I know the feeling," I say quietly. "Even with my broken survival instincts, the thought of being swallowed by that endless dark again shook me to the core."

 "It wasn't just nonexistence that frightened me," Leila writes, her fingers hesitating as the letters form. "Do you remember what we talked about in the caves? About what would have happened if the Magium had erased you during the God of Time trials? How I would have spent the rest of my life trying to convince everyone—and myself—that you were real, and not a phantom born of grief? That was the terror that paralyzed me. Surviving into a world where only I remembered you... that would have been unbearable."

As the last glyph fades, her hand trembles slightly against her knee. An anxious vulnerability lingers in her dark eyes.

choice("Reassure her that I'm not going anywhere, but don't hold her hand.", B3-Ch08b-Skeptical, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Skeptical, v_b3_ch8_heartfelt = 0)
choice("Hold her hand and reassure her that I'm not going anywhere.", B3-Ch08b-Skeptical, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Skeptical, v_b3_ch8_heartfelt = 1) if (v_b3_ch3_plans != 5)
choice("Hold her hand and reassure her that I'm not going anywhere.", B3-Ch08b-Oblivious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Oblivious) if (v_b3_ch3_plans == 5)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Skeptical
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 1) {
 "Do not worry," I say gently, placing my hand over hers where it rests on the cold stone. "I am not going anywhere."

"Oh? Have you already devised a foolproof countermeasure against the Magium?" Leila writes with a playful glint, gently slipping her hand free.

"Naturally," I say. "The entity never stood a chance. My terror was purely tactical misdirection to bait it into overextending. If you doubt my credentials, ask Arraka—she named me the prime candidate for primordial manipulation herself."

"Now that you mention it, she did endorse you as the only viable choice," Leila writes, playing along with the bravado. "I harbored doubts initially, but your confidence is undeniable. When do you intend to unleash your secret arts and strike down the entity once and for all?"

"All in good time," I say with a conspiratorial wink. "Revealing my ultimate technique before the climax would spoil the theatricality."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 0) {
 "Do not worry," I tell her softly. "I am not going anywhere."

"Oh? Have you already devised a foolproof countermeasure against the Magium?" Leila writes with a playful glint.

"Naturally," I say. "The entity never stood a chance. My terror was purely tactical misdirection to bait it into overextending. If you doubt my credentials, ask Arraka—she named me the prime candidate for primordial manipulation herself."

"Now that you mention it, she did endorse you as the only viable choice," Leila writes, playing along with the bravado. "I harbored doubts initially, but your confidence is undeniable. When do you intend to unleash your secret arts and strike down the entity once and for all?"

"All in good time," I say with a conspiratorial wink. "Revealing my ultimate technique before the climax would spoil the theatricality."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 0 || v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 1) {
 "My apologies," Leila writes with a quiet laugh. "I almost jeopardized your master strategy out of idle curiosity. I should have known better."

"You are forgiven, provided you keep this classified from Daren and Kate," I say. "I say this purely to protect operational security, of course, and not because I would perish from embarrassment if they heard a single word."

"Your secrets are safe with me," Leila writes, her smile bright in the dim light.

Muffled voices and the scrape of gear echo from the sleeping quarters down the hall, signaling the end of the watch.

"We should head back," Leila writes, glancing toward the sound. "The encampment is waking."

"Right," I say, pushing myself up from the stone floor. "A long march ahead."

 Leila rises beside me, and as we walk, a band of glowing blue script hovers smoothly beside my shoulder, matching my stride.

"I enjoyed tonight," Leila writes. "I am glad we shared this watch. Perhaps we can find another quiet hour before we leave the city."

"I would like that," I say. "We will make time, watch or no watch."

"Let us hope so," Leila writes.

When we reach the quarters, the others are already rolling bedrolls and strapping on their packs. The moment we step through the threshold, they hoist their gear, ready for the muster.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Oblivious
TEXT:

 "Do not worry," I tell her gently, placing my hand over hers on the cold rock. "I am not going anywhere."

Leila looks up in surprise, her eyes wide for a heartbeat before a soft smile reaches her lips. She turns her palm upward, weaving her slender fingers through mine against the stone.

"I know," Leila writes simply, and for a long moment we sit together in the quiet corridor, hands intertwined while the lantern flame flickers.

Fresh script materializes before my eyes, the cerulean letters slightly uneven and blurrier than before.

"There is something I have wanted to ask for some time, but the right moment never came," the writing says, Leila's gaze wavering with nervous hesitation. "I keep thinking about what you said days ago—that you would gladly return to Varathia after the tournament if you received an official invitation..."

"Of course," I say. "I meant every word."

 "If I could secure such an invitation through my father's contacts..." Leila writes, her hand tightening slightly against mine, "would you consider taking me with you to the Western Continent, so I could travel alongside you on the journeys you described?"

"Without question," I tell her. "My investigative work might seem mundane compared to fighting dragons and stillwaters, but I am sure Daren and Hadrik would welcome—"

"Actually—" Leila writes in sudden, oversized characters, blood rushing to her face in a crimson wave.

"Actually," she rewrites, the letters shrinking to a delicate, trembling script, "I was not asking about the group. I was hoping it might be... just the two of us?"

The vulnerability in her expression leaves no room for misunderstanding. Even with my dense skull, missing her intent now would require deliberate blindness.

choice(""I got it. Just the two of us, then. It's a promise."", B3-Ch08b-Leila5, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Leila5, v_b3_ch8_heartfelt = 2)
choice(""I'm sorry, I... can't do that to my friends. They'd feel betrayed if I didn't invite them."", B3-Ch08b-Leila5, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Leila5, v_b3_ch8_heartfelt = 3)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Leila5
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch8_heartfelt,1) if (v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 2)
achievement("A heartfelt promise",v_ac_b3_ch8_heartfelt)
#if(v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 2) {
 "I understand. Just the two of us, then. It is a promise."

Her face lights up with radiant relief. She clasps my hand with both of hers, squeezing hard, her dark eyes shining with unfiltered warmth.

"A promise!" Leila writes, beaming, and before I can anticipate the motion, she leans in and presses a soft, impulsive kiss to my lips.

The moment she pulls back and the reality of what she did registers, color floods her cheeks from collar to hairline. She averts her gaze, suddenly overwhelmed by self-consciousness. Before I can find my voice, she releases my hand, scrambles to her feet, and casts a hurried sentence into the air.

"I think the others are stirring," the blue writing says while her eyes remain fixed on the stone floor. "I will see if anyone needs assistance with gear!"

 "Leila, wait—" I start.

She takes two hasty strides toward the quarters, then stops dead, realizing that fleeing down the corridor will look as though she regrets the kiss.

Still flushed to the tips of her ears, she turns back to face me, her lips curving into a genuine, heartfelt smile.

"Thank you, Barry, for taking the watch with me," Leila writes. "I treasured our time tonight. I hope we can share another quiet moment soon."

"I treasured it as well," I say, rising to my feet. "Go ahead. I am right behind you."

 Leila nods, then hurries toward our quarters while I shoulder my pack.

Just the two of us.

Until tonight, I refused to contemplate a life where I failed to win the tournament or become a mage. Yet looking down the quiet stone passage now, that future no longer feels barren.

When I step into the quarters, the party is already on their feet, adjusting buckles and straps.

Leila glances my way as I enter, but the moment our eyes meet, a fresh blush sweeps her cheeks and she quickly turns toward her father's workbench. No one notices her fluster—except Melindra, whose knowing smirk across the room tells me she caught every detail, though she keeps her counsel for now.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_heartfelt == 3) {
 "I'm sorry, I... can't do that to my friends. They'd feel betrayed if I didn't invite them."

"Oh..." Leila writes, her fingers slowly loosening around mine as her gaze drifts to the stone floor. "You are... right, of course. They would want to share in the journey. It is better that way..."

After a heavy moment of silence, she withdraws her hand entirely and stands up without looking at me, new script forming in the air.

"I hear the others stirring," the writing says quietly. "We should return."

"Right," I say with hesitant regret. "Let us head back."

We walk the corridor in silence, Leila keeping a step ahead of me with her face turned away.

 "I... truly valued our time tonight," I tell her gently as we walk. "Perhaps we can share another watch sometime."

Leila halts. I stop beside her, waiting for the blue light.

"I valued it as well," Leila writes after a few seconds, turning to face me.

She offers her familiar smile, but beneath the composure, a quiet ache lingers in her eyes that she cannot entirely disguise.

 "Thank you for the watch, Barry," Leila writes.

"You are welcome," I say quietly, as she turns and leads the way into the quarters.

Neither of us speaks another word. When we enter, our companions are already packing bedrolls and tightening buckles, ready for the dawn muster.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Hadrik2
TEXT:

 "In that case, we can hold a three-man watch tonight," I say. "Daren, does that suit you?"

"I have no objection," Daren says, buckling his sword belt.

"Well now," Hadrik rumbles, a broad grin splitting his dark beard. "Two grown men offering to share a watch over drink on the very same evening. I must have been a miserable sight today. That ends right now. Daren, Barry, brace yourselves—we are holding a proper northern drinking bout tonight. No retreats!"

"If your bout means measured sips spaced across several hours, I agree," Daren says calmly.

"Hahaha!" Hadrik's laugh bounces off the stone. "Always the legend! You will see sense once the first bottle breathes. Come along—my pack is heavy with vintage."

After Kate and Leila offer to take the second watch, Hadrik, Daren, and I make our way down the gallery to a secluded bend where blast concussions cleared every cerulean vein from the walls. We sit cross-legged in a circle on the cold stone floor, and Hadrik unfastens his heavy leather backpack, pulling out one dark glass bottle after another.

"Good gods, man," Daren says as Hadrik sets down a sixth bottle of dark ale. "Just how much cargo space did your enchanter weave into that pack?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Hadrik3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Hadrik3)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Hadrik3
TEXT:

 "Enough to see us through any campaign," Hadrik says, grinning as he retrieves a seventh flagon.

"You have consumed a bottle a day since we crossed the border," Daren remarks, eyeing the row. "You must have purchased an entire cellar before boarding the ships."

"Several cellars," Hadrik says, resting the pack against the rock and passing each of us a heavy bottle. "I always stock up heavily whenever I pass through the dwarven highlands. Nothing brewed on the southern continents holds a candle to northern grain."

"Most enchanters limit dimensional storage arrays to prevent spatial compression failures," Daren notes. "I reinforce the boundary seams on the gear I enchant, but cheaper work tends to rupture under excess mass."

"That is why I paid an artisan's fee in gold," Hadrik says, popping the wax seal with his thumb. "The lining dampens impact so the glass never shatters, even when I take a hammer blow to the spine. Tailor-made for a giant who enjoys his drink. But enough theory—the throat parches. To good company!"

 "To good company," we echo, clinking the heavy glass rims before taking a drink. The ale hits with a deep, smoky maltiness and a fierce, warming burn that spreads immediately to the chest.

"You will have to drink deeper than that if you want to keep me company," Hadrik says, wiping foam from his mustache. "Those bottles look untouched."

"Unlike giants, humans do not possess half a century of alcohol tolerance built into our livers," I remind him. "You have been drinking highland brew since before my grandfather was born."

"Lost count of the decades, honestly," Hadrik chuckles. "Though even now, my tolerance is modest next to proper mountain dwarves. Those short folk have constitutions forged from elven steel. Anything milder than dwarven ale tastes like rain water to them."

 "Have northern merchants ever considered exporting the vintage abroad?" Daren asks, taking another slow sip. "In the western cities, a cask of genuine dwarven ale would fetch a small fortune in silver."

"Dwarves rarely leave their valleys for long," Hadrik explains, leaning back against the rock. "They visit foreign ports for trade or seasonal work, but they have no interest in establishing foreign mercantile houses. And southern traders rarely brave the northern passes. In practice, the only way outsiders ever taste the brew is when a wandering dwarf uncorks a bottle in a local tavern. That is how the legend spread, even if the drink remains nearly impossible to buy."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Hadrik4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Hadrik4)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Hadrik4
TEXT:

 "Fascinating," Daren says, swirling the dark liquid in his bottle. "I had assumed climate alone kept them north, unlike the western migrations from my homeland when maritime routes opened."

"Cold is part of it," Hadrik says, "but mostly it is the fellowship. Among the mountain clans, you are never a stranger for long. It is a shame more giants cannot see past old grudges. Centuries have passed since the wars between our peoples ended, yet most giants still regard dwarves with narrow suspicion. Even someone like Gontrok, who could shift down to their stature and share a table, still acts with the same arrogant—"

He stops mid-sentence, the bottle freezing halfway to his mouth.

"Damn it all," Hadrik mutters, his brow furrowing as he stares down at the stone floor. "I am speaking of him as if he were still walking the earth. What is wrong with me? I have barely touched the ale and I am already slipping into old habits."

 "You fought on the same battlefields for years before the falling out," I say quietly. "Reflexes run deep."

"We were never friends," Hadrik says, his voice dropping an octave, heavy and unvarnished. "Comrades-in-arms under the same banner, at best. But the way he died... Eiden turning an honorable duel into a slaughter, blowing his head apart, and then the Magium erasing his hand from history so the corpse looks like a mutilated stranger... I would not wish that on the bitterest enemy I ever faced. A giant warrior reduced to an unrecognizable carcass in a forgotten cavern."

 "Would you like us to retrieve his remains before we leave the city?" Daren asks softly. "We could see him given a proper burial under open sky."

"No," Hadrik says after a long silence, shaking his head. "I despise the dishonor of how he fell, but I owe Gontrok no debts. He made his choices, chose his masters, and paid the toll. That is where it ends."

He takes a long, slow draught from the flagon, setting the glass against the granite with a dull clink.

"Enough grim talk," Hadrik says, forcing a broader grin as he shakes his dark hair. "You two came here to lift my spirits. Go on, then—give it your best shot."

choice(""Did you ever hear the joke about the rock golem and the three-legged trampler?"", B3-Ch08b-Hadrik5, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Hadrik5, v_b3_ch8_three = 2)
choice(""Let's talk about your love life, then. How are things with Arraka going?"", B3-Ch08b-Hadrik5, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Hadrik5, v_b3_ch8_three = 3)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Hadrik5
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch8_friend,1) if (v_b3_ch8_three == 3)
achievement("A friend in need",v_ac_b3_ch8_friend)
#if(v_b3_ch8_three == 2) {
 "Did you ever hear the joke about the rock golem and the three-legged trampler?"

"Come now, Barry," Hadrik says with a chuckle. "Every taproom from here to the northern seas has heard that chestnut ten times over."

"How about a round of cards to pass the watch?" Daren suggests. "Something other than Met-Zek, if possible—we have thoroughly exhausted that deck."

"Now you are speaking my language," Hadrik says, brightening. "I have a northern trick-taking game that should keep your minds sharp. Let me lay out the suits."

For the next few hours we pass the time trading hands, sharing tavern tales, and keeping watch on the corridor.

 Hadrik tries more than once to coax us into matching him draught for draught, but Daren and I hold firm, keeping our wits clear with measured sips that leave only a comfortable warmth behind the eyes. As the watch concludes, we return to the quarters to wake Kate and Leila for their shift, then roll into our blankets.

When I wake several hours later, my head is clear and rested. Our companions are already on their feet packing gear, and soon after, Kate and Leila return from the watch post, ready for the muster.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_three == 3) {
 "Let's talk about your love life, then. How are things with Arraka going?"

Hadrik's booming laugh rolls down the tunnel, shaking the dust from the ceiling timbers.

"Now that is the proper spirit!" he roars, stroking his beard with a grin. "Let me see..."

"To be frank," Hadrik continues, "she and I have barely exchanged three words today. She spent half her time locked in quarantine with Daren, though. What do you say, Daren? Should I be watching my flank?"

"If you want to court the banshee, she is all yours," Daren says with a dry sigh. "After spending hours drilling aura containment while her screeching echoes in my skull, I value every breath of silence I can find. I have no idea how Illuna survived centuries in her proximity."

 "You complain," Hadrik says with a wink, "yet you two argue like old comrades now. In the beginning, you would not even look at the casing without reaching for your hilt."

"I will admit she has become more focused since the trials with the God of Time," Daren concedes, looking at his bottle. "She behaves less like a caged menace and more like a demanding coordinator. Taking down the pantheon must matter to her beyond anything else if she is willing to rein in her cruelty to keep us alive."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Ending) if (v_b3_ch8_three == 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch08b-Hadrik6, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08b-Hadrik6) if (v_b3_ch8_three == 3)


ID: B3-Ch08b-Hadrik6
TEXT:

 "I am not sure she changed so much as recovered her original purpose," I say. "Five thousand years ago, before she was trapped beneath a mountain, stuffed into animal vessels, and bound inside gold plating, she had a single-minded objective: tearing down the gods who cast her out. We saw her past self during the ritual—she was cold, lethal, and completely without theatrics. Her bizarre cruelty and dark humor were habits she cultivated out of sheer boredom once she realized she was stranded on this plane. Now that revenge is within reach again, her old discipline is resurfacing."

"Even during her wilder days," Hadrik says thoughtfully, "she showed glimpses of decency. She withheld the details of my trial with the God of Time until I chose to speak of it myself. I half expected her to weaponize it for a laugh the moment we escaped the temple. That counted for something."

 "Preferential treatment," I say with a grin. "Your romance is blossoming whether you admit it or not."

"Hah!" Hadrik bellows. "Perhaps I should commission matching iron bands after all!"

We spend the rest of the watch trading stories and passing the cards, the fire in the ale keeping the chill of the cavern at bay.

 When the watch concludes, Daren and I have finished less than a bottle each, leaving our senses sharp and rested. We return to our quarters to wake Kate and Leila for the second watch, then turn in for several hours of deep sleep.

I wake to the clatter of gear as the encampment stirs. Kate and Leila return from the watch post, and we all assemble before the doorway, packs hoisted and weapons checked.

